// [QL] R25 parallax showcase - a stone corridor.
//
// qltest_bump asks "is the shading correct" and uses flat grey and abstract
// sheets to do it. This one asks the question the whole line of work started
// from: does a low-polygon rock read as rock.
//
// WHY A CORRIDOR, AND WHY YOU WALK DOWN IT. Parallax offset is proportional to
// how grazing your view is, and head-on it is nearly a no-op by construction.
// A panel on a facing wall - which is what qltest_bump gives you - is close to
// the worst place to judge it. Here every surface is long, you pass it at a
// shallow angle, and the floor is the best showcase in any game because a floor
// is never seen any other way.
//
// WHAT IS WHERE
//
//   floor            cobble      rounded stones with deep gaps. Every gap is a
//                                hole the view ray has to climb out of.
//   both walls       stoneblock  irregular masonry, deeply recessed joints.
//                                Irregular on purpose - a regular grid reads as
//                                brick, and the joints stop looking like depth.
//   the boulders     boulder     ridged noise, so the creases are creases. These
//                                are ordinary axis-aligned blocks, which is
//                                exactly the seven-polygon rock R20 started
//                                from. Whether they read as rock is the test.
//   the strip        gravel      deliberately shallow, as the control. Parallax
//                                should be nearly invisible here; if it pops as
//                                hard as the cobble, the depth is running off
//                                something other than the height field.
//
//   behind the spawn             the four abstract sheets from qltest_bump, as
//                                a calibration bay: deep wells, channels, eight
//                                terraces, hemispheres. The terraces are the only
//                                thing here readable as a number rather than an
//                                impression - at the right depth the risers meet
//                                the treads, too deep and it shears.
//
// The silhouette is still flat. Every boulder is a box and will read as a box
// against the ceiling, because nothing here moves a vertex - that is R21 and it
// is parked. What is being judged is whether the FACES read as stone.
//
// Lights alternate sides and sit off the centre line, so nothing is lit head-on
// and a highlight sweeps as you walk. A light square in front of a surface
// cannot produce a moving highlight, and a moving highlight is most of what the
// eye reads as relief.
//
// Dim - 250, not the 700 the first qltest_bump used. At full white a modulation
// can only darken and half its range is invisible.
//
// Needs -deluxe, which build-testmap.sh passes. Without a deluxemap none of
// this does anything: a lightmap records how much light arrived, not from where.

